Why: SplitDatatype, which splits a STORE or COPY of a composite into its
fields, backs its root pointer up to a pointer to the containing structure
(RootPointer::backUpPointer) and then builds the field pointers from that
structure's layout (buildPointers), reducing an offset past its end modulo
its size. An incomplete structure has size 0: a struct the prototypes refer
to by tag without defining it, such as glibc's DIR (`typedef struct
__dirstream DIR;`, opaque in glibc's own headers). A function storing
through a DIR * (static glibc's opendir) then divides by zero: SIGFPE
natively, and a "remainder by zero" trap in the wasm, after which the
module's state cannot be trusted. This refuses to back up to a pointer to a type of size 0, so the
split stays at the stored value's own pointer. It cannot be done in host/:
the type must stay incomplete for the prototypes to print it by name.
Upstream: not reported yet; upstream's master (2026-10-18) divides the
same way in SplitDatatype::buildPointers.

--- a/cpp/subflow.cc
+++ b/cpp/subflow.cc
@@ -2119,6 +2119,8 @@
